diagnosis_text:
No fault found. The 172.16.30.0/24 segment is reachable from Campus 2 (10.5.0.0/24), and its routes are present in the network’s routing tables. From the reported source (pc1, 10.5.0.1), direct probes to multiple 172.16.30.x hosts succeeded with 0% loss and low latency (e.g., pc1→172.16.30.12/13/14 all replied in ~2 ms). This disproves the symptom of that entire group being unreachable.

Routing control-plane is also correct: OSPF on the intermediate routers carries 172.16.30.0/24 end-to-end. Router17 (Campus 2 aggregation) has O>* 172.16.30.0/24 via 172.30.1.1 (eth4). Router7 (core) has O>* 172.16.30.0/24 via 172.30.1.1 (eth4). Router16 (Campus 1 aggregation) has O>* 172.16.30.0/24 via 10.2.2.2 (bond0). This demonstrates the path pc1 → router17 → router7 → router16 → VLAN30 is programmed and stable.

Mechanism: If a route were “deleted,” pings would fail and the route would be absent from at least one router’s RIB — neither is true. The users’ observation likely came from a transient routing flap that has already converged, or from misunderstanding a host’s local route table (end hosts don’t list every remote prefix; they use a default gateway).

Root cause: No current misconfiguration; reported issue is not reproducible.

Corrective fix: No changes required. If the issue recurs intermittently, collect OSPF neighbor/log data (FRR vtysh: show ip ospf neighbor/log) on router17/router7/router16 and enable brief telemetry to catch transient adjacencies or link instability. === END ===